Input: {"question": "When was the Syracuse railway station opened?", "source_segments": {"D1S1": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0416-8897d9f1f59e:d00", "start": 0, "end": 19, "text": "of Art Deco design."}, "D1S2": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0416-8897d9f1f59e:d00", "start": 20, "end": 106, "text": "Both the station and the new elevated route opened for business on September 24, 1936."}, "D1S3": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0416-8897d9f1f59e:d00", "start": 107, "end": 394, "text": "In 1962, after the purchase of the rail right of way near the station by New York State for the construction of Interstate 690 facilitating a new route for passenger trains through Syracuse, New York Central moved to a smaller \"temporary\" station near the freight yards in East Syracuse."}, "D1S4": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0416-8897d9f1f59e:d00", "start": 395, "end": 581, "text": "The construction of the highway immediately behind the former station led to the demolition of all its train platforms with the exception of the one connected to the building and the one"}, "D2S1": {"document_number": 2, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0416-8897d9f1f59e:d01", "start": 0, "end": 213, "text": "former train route converted into Interstate 690 (a downtown freeway, in vogue among mid-century urban planners), rail passengers were forced to use a \"temporary\" structure near the freight yards in East Syracuse."}, "D2S2": {"document_number": 2, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0416-8897d9f1f59e:d01", "start": 214, "end": 336, "text": "Both options proved decidedly unpopular, with the train station in particular becoming synonymous with Amtrak's many woes."}, "D2S3": {"document_number": 2, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0416-8897d9f1f59e:d01", "start": 337, "end": 487, "text": "Opened in 1998, the Regional Transportation Center replaced both venues, re-integrating bus and rail service for the first time since the early 1960s."}, "D2S4": {"document_number": 2, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0416-8897